<DocsLayout title="Directory Lookup for Services">
  <h1>Directory Lookup for Services</h1>
  <p>
    <code>LookupUsers</code> resolves a batch of email addresses to the
    accounts they name in one project, whether or not those accounts have
    ever used the calling service. It is authorized by a machine credential
    that can do nothing else: a <strong>directory reader</strong> key.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use it instead of giving a service an admin account. An admin session can
    list, edit and delete every user; a directory reader key can only resolve
    addresses you already know to the minimal public profile of the
    <em>active</em> accounts they name, in one project.
  </p>

  <aside role="note" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4 text-sm">
    <strong>Postgres deployments only.</strong> Directory keys are project
    credentials, which live in the control plane. On a build without one
    (the memory and SQLite drivers) <code>LookupUsers</code> returns
    <code>UNIMPLEMENTED</code>. The <code>directory_reader</code> kind needs
    migration 0033: run <code>identity migrate</code>, or set
    <code>GATEWAY_POSTGRES_AUTO_MIGRATE</code>, before minting one. The boot
    log says which applies: <code>directory_lookup_enabled</code>, with the
    effective rate limit, or <code>directory_lookup_disabled</code>.
  </aside>

  <h2>What the key can and cannot do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It authorizes <code>LookupUsers</code> and no other RPC. Presented to
      anything else — as <code>X-Directory-Key</code> or as a Bearer token — it
      is ignored, and the call is answered as if no credential were present
      (<code>UNAUTHENTICATED</code> on every user-management RPC).
    </li>
    <li>
      It is bound to the project it was minted for. Every lookup reads that
      project's users, whatever <code>Host</code> or
      <code>X-Project-Key</code> the request carries. It is not a project key
      either: sent as <code>X-Project-Key</code>, or as the hosted OAuth
      routes' <code>project_key</code> parameter, it selects no project.
    </li>
    <li>
      Matching is exact, ignoring case — the comparison sign-in uses. No
      prefix, substring or domain search, so the directory cannot be listed
      through it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Only <strong>active</strong> accounts are returned. Invited,
      deactivated, suspended, and pending-consent or pending-deletion accounts
      are omitted, as are addresses with no account; an address is absent
      rather than an error.
    </li>
    <li>
      Only accounts whose owner has <strong>verified</strong> the address are
      returned while <code>GATEWAY_AUTH_REQUIRE_VERIFIED_EMAIL</code> is on,
      which is the default. Anyone can sign up with an address they do not
      own, so without this rule the lookup would return whoever registered it
      first. With the setting off, unverified accounts are returned too, with
      <code>email_verified: false</code>, and such an entry proves nothing
      about who owns the address; with it on, every entry has
      <code>email_verified: true</code>. See
      <a href="#verified-email">Verified email</a> below.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each entry carries <code>id</code> (the account's <code>sub</code>),
      <code>email</code>, <code>name</code>, <code>avatar_url</code> and
      <code>email_verified</code>.
      Phone numbers, roles, MFA and login state are never returned.
    </li>
    <li>
      A batch holds 1–100 addresses of at most 320 bytes each. Calls are
      rate-limited per client IP by
      <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_DIRECTORY_PER_IP</code> (default 120) per
      <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS</code> window (default 60 s),
      on every transport identity serves.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Sizing the rate limit</h3>
  <p>
    The limit counts calls per client IP, and a service usually reaches the
    identity server from a few egress addresses — so for a machine caller it
    is effectively a per-service limit, shared by every instance behind one
    address. Set it to the peak number of <code>LookupUsers</code> calls all
    of those instances make in one window, with headroom; each call resolves
    up to 100 addresses, so batch before you raise it. Unlike the other
    per-IP limits it cannot be switched off: unset, zero or a value that is
    not an integer leaves the default of 120 in force, and a negative value
    fails the boot.
  </p>
  <p>
    A throttled call is refused before it reaches the lookup, with HTTP
    <code>429 Too Many Requests</code>, a <code>Retry-After</code> header
    giving the window in seconds (<code>60</code> with the default window)
    and a plain-text body. A Connect client reports it as
    <code>UNAVAILABLE</code>. Back off for at least that long; the count
    resets when the window rolls over.
  </p>
  <p>
    A host that embeds identity and serves it on its own gRPC server through
    <code>RegisterGRPC</code> (see
    <a href="https://github.com/elloloop/identity/blob/main/docs/embedding.md"><code>docs/embedding.md</code></a>)
    gets the same limit there, drawn from the same per-IP budget as the HTTP
    handler, so serving both does not double what a caller may spend. A
    throttled native gRPC call fails with <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code> and
    carries the wait in its <code>retry-after</code> response metadata. The
    caller's address is the connection's peer, or the first untrusted
    <code>x-forwarded-for</code> hop when the peer is in
    <code>GATEWAY_TRUSTED_PROXIES</code> — the rule the HTTP handler uses.
  </p>

  <h2>Mint a key</h2>
  <p>
    Directory keys are minted by the platform operator with the existing
    <code>AdminCreateProjectCredential</code> RPC, authenticated by
    <code>X-Admin-Secret</code> (<code>GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET</code>), with
    <code>kind</code> set to <code>directory_reader</code>:
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`curl -sS -X POST https://auth.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/AdminCreateProjectCredential \\
  -H "X-Admin-Secret: $GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"projectId": "<project-id>", "kind": "directory_reader"}'

# {"credentialId":"8f3c…","publicId":"dk_4b1e…","rawKey":"dk_4b1e….c9a0…"}`}
  />
  <p>
    <code>rawKey</code> is shown <strong>once</strong>; only a hash of its
    secret half is stored. Put it in the calling service's secret store and
    record <code>credentialId</code> — you need it to revoke the key.
  </p>

  <h2>Look up users</h2>
  <p>
    Present the raw key in the <code>X-Directory-Key</code> header, from
    server-side code only. It is deliberately not the
    <code>Authorization</code> header, and it is left out of the CORS
    allow-list, so a browser cannot send it cross-origin: the key never
    belongs in a web or mobile client.
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`curl -sS -X POST https://auth.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/LookupUsers \\
  -H "X-Directory-Key: $DIRECTORY_KEY" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"emails": ["alice@corp.com", "bob@corp.com"]}'

# {"users":[{"id":"u_31…","email":"alice@corp.com","name":"Alice","avatarUrl":"https://…","emailVerified":true}]}`}
  />
  <p>
    Results come back in request order, one entry per distinct address that
    names an account the lookup may return. Addresses that differ only in case
    are the same address and produce one entry. Because omitted addresses
    leave no gap, match each entry to your request by its <code>email</code>,
    never by position.
  </p>
  <p>
    A missing, wrong, revoked or non-directory key is
    <code>UNAUTHENTICATED</code>; an empty batch, a blank address, an address
    longer than 320 bytes or more than 100 addresses is
    <code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="verified-email">Verified email</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GATEWAY_AUTH_REQUIRE_VERIFIED_EMAIL</code> (default
    <code>true</code>) is the deployment-wide rule that an unverified address
    cannot be used: password sign-up issues no session until the address is
    verified, and password sign-in is refused. The directory lookup follows
    the same rule, so an account created by signing up with an address is not
    returned until its owner confirms the verification link.
  </p>
  <p>
    Set it to <code>false</code> only if your deployment accepts unverified
    addresses everywhere. The lookup then returns those accounts as well, and
    <code>email_verified</code> tells the two apart.
  </p>
  <p>
    With the setting off, a caller <strong>must not</strong> treat an entry
    with <code>email_verified: false</code> as proof that the account owns the
    address, and must not authorize anything on that basis — granting access
    addressed to that email, merging it with another record, or sending it
    something meant for the address's owner. Anyone can register an address
    they do not control, so such an entry says only that someone signed up
    with it. Act on it only once <code>email_verified</code> is
    <code>true</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Revoke a key</h2>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`curl -sS -X POST https://auth.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/AdminRevokeProjectCredential \\
  -H "X-Admin-Secret: $GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{"projectId": "<project-id>", "credentialId": "<credential-id>"}'`}
  />
  <p>
    A revoked directory key is refused on its next lookup: every lookup
    reads the credential afresh. Revoking an already-revoked key is a no-op;
    a credential id the project does not own is <code>NOT_FOUND</code>.
    To rotate, mint the new key, deploy it, then revoke the old one.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>AdminRevokeProjectCredential</code> revokes project credentials of
    every kind, not only directory keys, but the other kinds are not refused
    as promptly. A publishable, secret or mTLS key presented as
    <code>X-Project-Key</code> (or <code>project_key</code>) selects its project through a per-replica
    project-resolution cache that revocation does not clear, so each replica
    keeps resolving the key for up to
    <code>GATEWAY_PROJECT_RESOLUTION_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS</code> (default 30 s)
    after the revoke. Plan an incident response around that window.
  </p>

  <h2>Audit</h2>
  <p>
    Every event is recorded in the credential's project. Minting and revoking
    record <code>project_credential_created</code> and
    <code>project_credential_revoked</code>. Every lookup records
    <code>directory_lookup</code>, with the actor
    <code>credential:&lt;credential-id&gt;</code> and the number of addresses
    requested and matched — never the addresses themselves. A refused
    presentation of a real directory key (wrong secret, or revoked) is
    recorded with <code>success=false</code> and a <code>reason</code>.
    Presenting the public id of another kind of credential, such as a
    publishable key, is refused without an audit row. The raw key and its
    hash are never logged.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
